[?25l[0m[38;5;141m> [0mTwo bugs were found and fixed:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mBug 1: Missing [38;5;10mSPX_hash_message[0m export (in [38;5;10mlib.rs[0m)[22m[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The test runner dynamically loads the symbol [38;5;10mSPX_hash_message[0m from the shared library. In the C code, [38;5;10mhash_message[0m is [38;5;10m#define[0md to [38;5;10mSPX_hash_message[0m via [38;5;10mSPX_NAMESPACE(hash_message)[0m. The Rust translation had no [38;5;10m#[no_mangle] extern "C"[0m function with that name — it only exported [38;5;10mcrypto_sign_*[0m functions. I added an [38;5;10mSPX_hash_message[0m wrapper in [38;5;10mlib.rs[0m that delegates to [38;5;10mhash_blake::hash_message[0m.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mBug 2: Incorrect [38;5;10m*8[0m bit conversion in [38;5;10mhash_blake.rs[0m (in [38;5;10mgen_message_random[0m and [38;5;10mhash_message[0m)[22m[0m[0m
[0m[0m
This is the subtle one. The [38;5;10mblake256_update[0m function takes [38;5;10mdatalen[0m in [1mbits[22m (it internally does [38;5;10m>> 3[0m to convert to bytes). The C [38;5;10mblake256()[0m [3mwrapper[23m function correctly converts bytes→bits: [38;5;10mblake256_update(&S, in, inlen*8)[0m. However, the C [38;5;10mhash_message[0m and [38;5;10mgen_message_random[0m functions call [38;5;10mblake256_update[0m [1mdirectly[22m and pass raw byte counts (e.g., [38;5;10mSPX_N[0m = 16) without multiplying by 8. This means they only hash 2 bytes (16 bits) instead of 16 bytes — that's the actual C behavior.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The Rust translator saw that [38;5;10mblake256_update[0m expects bits and "helpfully" added [38;5;10m* 8[0m multiplications in [38;5;10mgen_message_random[0m and [38;5;10mhash_message[0m, making the Rust code hash 8× more data than the C code. Removing the [38;5;10m* 8[0m makes the Rust match the C behavior exactly.[0m[0m
